===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|moon-calf}}

===Etymology===
From {{compound|en|moon|calf}}, after a superstition that the moon caused abnormal fetal development.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈmuːnkɑːf/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|mooncalves}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|rare}} An abnormal [[mass]] within the uterus; a [[false]] [[conception]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|II|2|text=Thou art very Trinculo indeed! How camest thou to be the [[siege]] of this '''moon-calf'''? Can he vent Trinculos?}}
# A poorly-conceived [[idea]] or [[plan]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
# A [[dreamer]], someone [[absent-minded]] or distracted; a [[fool]], [[simpleton]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1902|author=w:John Kendrick Bangs|title=Olympian Nights|location=New York|publisher=Harper & Bros.|chapter=10|page=185|url=https://archive.org/details/olympiannights00banguoft
|passage=“{{...}} you’re a jobbernowl and a doodle, a maundering '''mooncalf''' and a blockheaded numps, a gaby and a loon; you’re a ''Hatter!''” I shrieked the last epithet.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1957|author=w:Ogden Nash|chapter=Come On In, The Senility Is Fine|title=You Can’t Get There From Here|location=Boston|publisher=Little, Brown & Co.|page=66|url=https://archive.org/details/youcantgettheref00nash
|passage=But I can think of no one but a '''mooncalf''' or a gaby<br>Who would trust their own child to raise a baby.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1978|author=w:Lawrence Durrell|title={{w|Livia (novel)|Livia}}|publisher=Faber & Faber|year_published=1992|series=Avignon Quintet|page=463
|passage=He slipped it softly onto her unresisting finger and, like the unwise '''moncalf''' he was, kissed it.}}

